From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Desidero <desidero@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 18:12:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(deploy): take a Databricks deployment's own settings
 from an overlay

A real Databricks Apps deployment needs values the tracked bundle cannot
hold: the workspace host, extras such as `kms`, app env vars, an OAuth
client secret from a secret scope, and config files beside app.py. Each
meant editing databricks.yml (twice, once per target's env list),
deploy.py or src/ by hand and deploying with --allow-dirty
(https://github.com/Desidero/omnigent/issues/21).

`deploy.py --overlay <file>` reads them from one untracked YAML file with
five optional keys: host, extras, env, secret_env and files.

- extras are added to the generated src/pyproject.toml.
- host, env and secret_env become a generated bundle fragment,
  overlay.generated.yml, which databricks.yml now includes by glob. It
  defines `<target>-overlay`, a copy of the target chosen by --target and
  --no-otel with the host replaced, the target's env list followed by
  the overlay's, and one app `secret` resource (READ) per secret-backed
  variable. Applying it after target selection is what removes the
  duplicated edit. The copy keeps the base target's root_path, so the
  deployment state is shared. Without bundle keys, or without an
  overlay, the base target deploys exactly as before.
- files are copied into src/ for the deploy and removed when deploy.py
  exits.

The file is checked before the build: unknown keys fail, a variable the
target already sets (OMNIGENT_FEATURES stays with --features) or a
secret-looking name under plain `env` is refused, and a file may not
replace anything in src/ or use a gitignored name, which the bundle sync
would skip. Only names are logged, never values. The overlay path
pattern (deploy/databricks/*.local.yml) and the generated fragment are
gitignored.

Not run against a workspace or the Databricks CLI. The tests cover
parsing, the generated target (built from the real databricks.yml), file
handling and the wiring through main(). Two things the design leans on
were read in the CLI's source instead: an `include` glob that matches
nothing is not an error (process_root_includes.go), and an app's
`resources` are merged by name (merge_apps.go). Binding the new target
to state created through the base target is untested.

Not upstream yet: not proposed, and there is no contribution branch for
it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Desidero <desidero@gmail.com>
